    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With finite element models, we investigate the evolution of fractures during the deformation history of an asymmetric anticline. The effect of material properties, slip on bedding surfaces, and overall compression of the fold in the response of the system are studied. The model uses large deformation friction contact elements to capture the response of existing fractures, the slip along bedding surfaces, and to better constraint the mechanical models. The numerical simulations demonstrate the sequence and the mode of deformation of bed-perpendicular fractures. We show that fractures located in the hinge are reactivated as joints and that the ones in the limbs are predominantly reactivated as reverse faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
